LIB_LOAD("ac",{"ac1":{"challenge":[{"q":"In which part of Nigeria is Osogun?","o":["Oyo State","Kano State","Rivers State","Borno State"],"why":"In Yorubaland."},{"q":"What happened to Osogun in 1821?","o":["It was raided and burned","It became a city","It flooded","It won a prize"],"why":"Ajayi was about twelve or thirteen."},{"q":"Who was captured with Ajayi?","o":["His mother, sisters and baby brother","Nobody","His teacher","His father only"],"why":"His family was taken together."},{"q":"What was Ajayi exchanged for?","o":["A horse","A cow","Some cloth","A canoe"],"why":"Treated like property."},{"q":"How many times was Ajayi sold?","o":["Again and again, to many owners","Once","Never","Only to his uncle"],"why":"Each time further from home."},{"q":"What is the Yoruba name for Lagos?","o":["Eko","Ibadan","Ife","Oyo"],"why":"A slave port in the 1820s."},{"q":"Who bought Ajayi at Lagos?","o":["Portuguese slave traders","His uncle","Missionaries","British sailors"],"why":"And put him on a ship."},{"q":"What frightened Ajayi when he first saw them?","o":["White men","Camels","Elephants","Lions"],"why":"He had never seen anyone like them."},{"q":"Where were slave ships usually heading?","o":["Across the Atlantic to the Americas","To Mecca","Up the Niger","To China"],"why":"To sell the captives."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"When my father and my mother forsake me, then the LORD will ___.\"","o":["take me up","leave me","punish me","forget me"],"why":"Psalm 27:10."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! Which famous Nigerian is this book about?","o":["Samuel Ajayi Crowther","Herbert Macaulay","Nnamdi Azikiwe","Wole Soyinka"],"why":"The first African Anglican bishop."},"choices":[{"title":"Learning hard history","q":"Your history teacher starts a lesson on the slave trade. Some classmates make jokes to avoid how uncomfortable it feels.","o":[{"t":"Listen respectfully and ask questions. These were real people, like Ajayi.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Remembering honestly honours the millions who suffered."},{"t":"Join the jokes.","f":"Ajayi was a boy your age. Some things are not for joking."},{"t":"Tune out. It was long ago.","f":"Understanding the past helps us build a better future."}]},{"title":"When your family is far away","q":"Your parents have travelled for work for three months, and you're staying with relatives. You miss them badly.","o":[{"t":"Tell God how you feel, write to them, and thank the people caring for you.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Psalm 27:10: the Lord takes us up."},{"t":"Shut yourself away and refuse to talk to anyone.","f":"Missing people is natural, but don't carry it alone."},{"t":"Decide nobody cares about you.","f":"Ajayi was far more alone than that, and God hadn't forgotten him."}]},{"title":"Treated like nothing","q":"Someone at school keeps calling you 'nobody' and says you'll never amount to anything.","o":[{"t":"Remember God calls you by name. Tell a trusted adult, and keep doing your best.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! The traders thought Ajayi was nobody. History disagrees."},{"t":"Believe them and stop trying.","f":"Ajayi went from a slave ship to a bishop's chair."},{"t":"Insult them back worse.","f":"That doesn't change what God says about you."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"osogun","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 1 of Ajayi Crowther's story in real life."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"🌱 God knows you before you know him, wherever you were born."},{"sc":"osogunraid","n":"💔 When terrible things happen, God is close to the broken-hearted."},{"sc":"barracoon","n":"🌦️ Ìgbà kan ń lọ, ìgbà kan ń bọ̀. Hard seasons don't last for ever."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"🏷️ You're never just a number. God calls you by name."}],"stops":{"ac1s1":{"story":[{"sc":"osogun","n":"About two hundred years ago, in the town of {Osogun} in Yorubaland, a baby boy was born. His parents named him {Ajayi}."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"Osogun is in today's Oyo State, Nigeria. It was a busy, happy town of farmers and weavers, surrounded by palm trees and red earth."},{"sc":"osogun","w":"afala","e":"smile","t":"Ajayi! Come and help me carry the yams. Your sisters are already at the farm!"},{"sc":"osogun","w":"ajayi","e":"big","t":"Coming, Mama!"},{"sc":"osogun","n":"Ajayi lived with his father, his mother Afala, his sisters and his baby brother. Years later, he remembered that time like this:"},{"sc":"letterdesk","w":"crowther","e":"smile","t":"I was in my native country, enjoying the comforts of father and mother, and the affectionate love of brothers and sisters."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"He didn't know the God of the Bible yet. He didn't know how to read. He had never seen the sea. He was just a boy, loved by his family."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"But God knew him. And God had plans for Ajayi that would one day change Nigeria and the whole of Africa."}],"quote":{"text":"I was in my native country, enjoying the comforts of father and mother, and the affectionate love of brothers and sisters.","who":"Samuel Ajayi Crowther, 1837","tag":"Crowther's words"},"lesson":{"title":"Known before you knew","text":"Ajayi was an ordinary Yoruba boy in an ordinary town. Nobody could have guessed what he would become. God sees every child, in every town, and he has plans for you too, even before you know him."},"think":"Ajayi's story starts in a small Nigerian town. What does that tell you about where great leaders can come from?","quiz":[{"q":"Where was Ajayi born?","o":["Osogun, in Yorubaland","Lagos","London","Freetown"],"why":"In today's Oyo State, Nigeria."},{"q":"What does the Yoruba name 'Ajayi' tell us?","o":["He was born face-down","He was a king's son","He was the first child","He was born at night"],"why":"It's a Yoruba birth name."},{"q":"What was his mother's name?","o":["Afala","Susan","Hannah","Asano"],"why":"Later she would be baptised as Hannah."},{"q":"How did Ajayi remember his early childhood?","o":["Enjoying the love of his family","Lonely and sad","At boarding school","On a ship"],"why":"A happy, loved home."},{"q":"What does Jeremiah 1:5 say God did before we were born?","o":["Knew us","Forgot us","Ignored us","Judged us"],"why":"God knew Ajayi before he was born."}]},"ac1s2":{"story":[{"sc":"osogun","n":"One morning early in 1821, when Ajayi was about twelve or thirteen, the people of Osogun were getting ready for the day."},{"sc":"osogunraid","n":"Suddenly there were shouts and the thunder of horses. Raiders had surrounded the town. In those years, wars were tearing Yorubaland apart, and captured people were sold as slaves."},{"sc":"osogunraid","w":"acraider","e":"stern","t":"Take them! Take everyone who can walk!"},{"sc":"osogunraid","n":"The houses were set on fire. People ran in every direction. Ajayi's family tried to escape into the bush, but the raiders caught them."},{"sc":"osogunraid","w":"afala","e":"worried","t":"Ajayi! Stay close to me! Hold your sister's hand!"},{"sc":"osogunraid","n":"Ajayi, his mother, his sisters and his baby brother were tied together with ropes around their necks, with many others from the town."},{"sc":"captivesroad","n":"They were marched away from Osogun. Ajayi looked back and saw his home in flames. He never saw his father again."},{"sc":"captivesroad","n":"This was the {slave trade}. It tore millions of families apart, in Nigeria and all over Africa. It is right to feel sad and angry about it. God hates injustice too."}],"quote":{"text":"The LORD is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart; and saveth such as be of a contrite spirit.","who":"Psalm 34:18","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God sees injustice","text":"The raid on Osogun was cruel and wrong. God never wanted it. The Bible says the Lord is close to the broken-hearted. When terrible things happen, it's okay to grieve, and to know that God sees and cares."},"think":"Why is it important to remember and learn about the slave trade, even though it's painful?","quiz":[{"q":"In what year was Osogun raided?","o":["1821","1921","1700","2001"],"why":"Early in the year."},{"q":"About how old was Ajayi?","o":["About twelve or thirteen","About five","About twenty","About thirty"],"why":"Just a boy."},{"q":"What happened to the houses of Osogun?","o":["They were set on fire","They were painted","They were sold","Nothing"],"why":"He saw his home in flames."},{"q":"How were the captives tied?","o":["With ropes around their necks","With gold chains","They weren't tied","With ribbons"],"why":"Families were led away together."},{"q":"What does Psalm 34:18 say?","o":["The Lord is near the broken-hearted","The Lord is far away","Be strong alone","Forget your pain"],"why":"God cares."}]},"ac1s3":{"story":[{"sc":"captivesroad","n":"The captives were taken to a market town. There, the hardest thing of all happened."},{"sc":"market","n":"Ajayi was taken away from his mother and sisters. He was traded for a horse."},{"sc":"market","w":"ajayi","e":"worried","t":"Mama! Mama! Where are you taking her?"},{"sc":"market","n":"A boy, swapped for an animal, as if he were a thing. It is one of the cruellest parts of his story."},{"sc":"market","n":"Over the next months, Ajayi was sold again and again, from one owner to another, each time further from home."},{"sc":"captivesroad","n":"He was hungry, frightened and alone. Later he wrote about how lost and miserable he felt. But he survived."},{"sc":"barracoon","n":"At last he was taken to {Eko}, the port we now call Lagos. There he was sold to Portuguese slave traders and locked in a pen by the lagoon with many others."},{"sc":"barracoon","n":"For the first time in his life, Ajayi saw the sea. And he saw white men, which frightened him, because he had never seen anyone like them."},{"sc":"barracoon","n":"He had lost his home, his family and his freedom. But he had not been forgotten. Not by God."}],"quote":{"text":"When my father and my mother forsake me, then the LORD will take me up.","who":"Psalm 27:10","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Never forgotten","text":"Ajayi was separated from his family and sold like property. But God had not lost track of him for a single day. Even when everyone else is gone, the Lord takes us up."},"think":"How do you think Ajayi kept going when he was alone and far from home? What helps you keep hoping in hard times?","proverb":{"text":"Ìgbà kan ń lọ, ìgbà kan ń bọ̀.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"One season goes, another season comes. Hard times do not last for ever."},"quiz":[{"q":"What was Ajayi traded for?","o":["A horse","A goat","A bag of salt","A gun"],"why":"As if he were a thing."},{"q":"What happened to Ajayi and his mother?","o":["They were separated","They stayed together","They escaped together","They went home"],"why":"The hardest thing of all."},{"q":"Where was Ajayi taken at last?","o":["Eko (Lagos)","Ibadan","Kano","Abuja"],"why":"A slave port by the lagoon."},{"q":"What did Ajayi see there for the first time?","o":["The sea and white men","Snow","A train","A car"],"why":"Everything was new and frightening."},{"q":"What does the Yoruba proverb say?","o":["One season goes, another comes","Never travel","Horses are fast","The sea is wide"],"why":"Hard times do not last for ever."}]},"ac1s4":{"story":[{"sc":"barracoon","n":"Ajayi stayed in the pen at Lagos for months. Then, in April 1822, the traders rowed the captives out to a ship called the Esperança Felix."},{"sc":"barracoon","w":"actrader","e":"stern","t":"Move! Below deck, all of you!"},{"sc":"nightsea","n":"Ajayi was pushed down into the dark, hot hold of the {slave ship}, packed tightly with more than a hundred and eighty other people. Chains clinked. Children cried."},{"sc":"nightsea","n":"The ship sailed out into the ocean. Ajayi had no idea where they were going, or what would happen to him there."},{"sc":"nightsea","w":"ajayi","e":"worried","t":"Where are they taking us? Will I ever see home again?"},{"sc":"nightsea","n":"Many ships like this one crossed the Atlantic to the Americas, where the captives were sold to work on farms and in mines for the rest of their lives."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"But this ship never got there. The very next day, sails appeared on the horizon. Big ships. Warships. And they were coming straight towards the slave ship."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"God was about to do something that Ajayi could never have imagined."}],"quote":{"text":"Fear not: for I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine.","who":"Isaiah 43:1","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Called by name","text":"On the slave ship, Ajayi was just one of many people in chains, with no name and no value to the traders. But to God, he was known by name. Nobody is just a number to God."},"think":"The traders treated Ajayi like property. What does Isaiah 43:1 say about how God sees each person?","quiz":[{"q":"What was the name of the slave ship?","o":["Esperança Felix","Mayflower","Dayspring","Titanic"],"why":"A Portuguese ship."},{"q":"When did it sail?","o":["April 1822","June 1900","December 1821","May 1850"],"why":"From the Lagos lagoon."},{"q":"How many people were packed into the ship?","o":["More than a hundred and eighty","Ten","Five thousand","Two"],"why":"In the dark, hot hold."},{"q":"What appeared the very next day?","o":["Warships","A storm","An island","Whales"],"why":"Coming straight towards them."},{"q":"What does Isaiah 43:1 say God does?","o":["Calls us by our name","Forgets our name","Changes our name to a number","Ignores us"],"why":"Thou art mine."}]}}},"ac2":{"challenge":[{"q":"Which navy rescued Ajayi?","o":["The Royal Navy","The Nigerian Navy","The Portuguese Navy","The US Navy"],"why":"It patrolled for slave ships."},{"q":"In which month and year did Ajayi reach Freetown?","o":["June 1822","April 1821","December 1825","January 1900"],"why":"As a free boy."},{"q":"What was Freetown founded for?","o":["As a home for freed Africans","As a prison","As a fort","As a market"],"why":"Thousands started new lives there."},{"q":"Who taught at Ajayi's school?","o":["Missionaries","Soldiers","Slave traders","Sailors"],"why":"In Bathurst village."},{"q":"What did Ajayi do within about six months?","o":["Learned to read","Built a house","Became a bishop","Sailed home"],"why":"His teachers were amazed."},{"q":"Where did Ajayi meet Jesus?","o":["Reading the Bible and learning from his teachers","In a dream only","On the slave ship","In London"],"why":"He came to believe for himself."},{"q":"When did Ajayi become 'Samuel Crowther'?","o":["At his baptism","At birth","At his wedding","When he became a bishop"],"why":"11 December 1825."},{"q":"What was the name of the college where Samuel was the first student?","o":["Fourah Bay College","Oxford University","Yaba College","Harvard"],"why":"In Freetown, 1827."},{"q":"Who was Asano?","o":["Samuel's wife, also rescued from slavery","His sister","His teacher","A queen"],"why":"They married in 1829."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Thy word is a ___ unto my feet.\"","o":["lamp","stone","road","shoe"],"why":"Psalm 119:105."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did Samuel keep all his life alongside his English name?","o":["His Yoruba name, Ajayi","His chains","A horse","A sword"],"why":"Samuel Ajayi Crowther: proudly both."},"choices":[{"title":"School feels boring","q":"It's Monday morning and you really don't feel like going to school. \"What's the point?\" you think.","o":[{"t":"Remember Ajayi had never seen a book until he was rescued, and go ready to learn.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Education is a gift many children still don't have."},{"t":"Pretend to be sick.","f":"That's wasting a treasure Ajayi would have loved."},{"t":"Go, but sleep through class.","f":"Being there isn't the same as learning."}]},{"title":"Ashamed of your language","q":"At a new school, some students laugh when you speak Yoruba, Igbo or Hausa. You think about never speaking it again.","o":[{"t":"Keep speaking it proudly at home and with friends. Your language is a gift.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Samuel Ajayi Crowther kept his Yoruba name and later wrote his language down for millions."},{"t":"Stop speaking it, even at home.","f":"Losing your language means losing part of your story."},{"t":"Mock other people's languages instead.","f":"Every language is part of God's world."}]},{"title":"Learn, then teach","q":"You finally understand fractions, but your younger cousin is struggling and about to give up.","o":[{"t":"Sit with them and explain it patiently.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Samuel learned, then taught others."},{"t":"Do their homework for them.","f":"That doesn't help them learn."},{"t":"Tell them they're slow.","f":"Samuel once couldn't read either."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"rescueships","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 2 of Ajayi Crowther's story in real life."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"⚓ God's heart is for freedom. Stand up for anyone being treated unjustly."},{"sc":"study","n":"📖 Treat learning as a treasure. Millions of children would love your chance."},{"sc":"church","n":"🏷️ Be proud of your name, your language and your people, and bring all of it to God."},{"sc":"college","n":"🎓 Learn, then teach. Pass on what you've received."}],"stops":{"ac2s1":{"story":[{"sc":"rescueships","n":"The warships belonged to Britain's {Royal Navy}. Britain had made the slave trade illegal in 1807, and these ships hunted for slave ships along the West African coast."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"Two of them, the Myrmidon and the Iphigenia, caught up with the Esperança Felix. British sailors climbed aboard. The slave traders were arrested."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"Down in the dark hold, the captives were terrified. They didn't know who these new white men were. Were things about to get even worse?"},{"sc":"rescueships","w":"acsailor","e":"smile","t":"It's all right. You're free now. Nobody is going to sell you."},{"sc":"rescueships","n":"The chains were taken off. The sailors gave them food and water and let them breathe fresh air on deck."},{"sc":"rescueships","w":"ajayi","e":"shock","t":"Free? Did he say free?"},{"sc":"seashore","n":"In June 1822, the ship reached {Freetown} in {Sierra Leone}, a town founded as a home for freed Africans. Ajayi stepped onto the shore a free boy."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"He had been captured, chained and sold. Now he was free. Ajayi didn't know the God who had rescued him yet. But he would soon."}],"quote":{"text":"He hath sent me to heal the brokenhearted, to preach deliverance to the captives.","who":"Luke 4:18","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Freedom is God's heart","text":"The slave ship was stopped, and the captives were set free. Jesus said he came to set captives free. God's heart is always for freedom: from chains, from injustice and from sin."},"think":"The captives were terrified even when rescue came, because they didn't know their rescuers. Why can it be hard to trust help when you've been hurt?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Britain make the slave trade illegal?","o":["1807","1907","1707","1977"],"why":"Its navy then hunted slave ships."},{"q":"Which ships stopped the slave ship?","o":["The Myrmidon and the Iphigenia","The Titanic and the Ark","The Dayspring and the Pearl","None"],"why":"British warships."},{"q":"How did the captives feel at first?","o":["Terrified","Excited","Bored","Angry"],"why":"They didn't know who their rescuers were."},{"q":"Where was Ajayi taken?","o":["Freetown, Sierra Leone","London","Lagos","Brazil"],"why":"A home for freed Africans."},{"q":"What did Jesus say he came to preach to captives?","o":["Deliverance","Punishment","Silence","Fear"],"why":"Luke 4:18."}]},"ac2s2":{"story":[{"sc":"freetown","n":"In Freetown, Ajayi was sent to live in a village called Bathurst, where {missionaries} ran a school for rescued children."},{"sc":"freetown","w":"acdavey","e":"smile","t":"Welcome, Ajayi. Here you'll learn to read and write. Would you like that?"},{"sc":"study","n":"Ajayi had never held a book in his life. He had never seen letters. But the moment he understood what reading was, he was hooked."},{"sc":"study","w":"ajayi","e":"big","t":"These marks make words? And the words can tell stories from far away?"},{"sc":"study","n":"He worked so hard that within about six months he could read. His teachers had never seen a pupil learn so fast."},{"sc":"study","n":"And the most important book he read was the Bible. In it, he met the God who had seen him in Osogun, in the slave market and on the ship."},{"sc":"study","n":"Ajayi was hungry to learn everything: English, maths, carpentry and more. That hunger for learning never left him for the rest of his life."}],"quote":{"text":"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path.","who":"Psalm 119:105","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Learning is a gift","text":"Ajayi went from never having seen a book to reading in about six months. He treated school as a treasure, not a chore. Your education is a gift that millions of children still don't have. Use it well."},"think":"Ajayi had never seen a book before he was rescued. How might that change the way you think about your own schoolbooks?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did Ajayi go to school?","o":["A mission school in Bathurst village","Oxford","Lagos","Nowhere"],"why":"Near Freetown."},{"q":"Had Ajayi ever seen a book before?","o":["No, never","Yes, many","Only one","Only comics"],"why":"He'd never seen letters."},{"q":"How long did it take him to learn to read?","o":["About six months","Ten years","One day","He never learned"],"why":"His teachers were amazed."},{"q":"What was the most important book Ajayi read?","o":["The Bible","A cookbook","A comic","A newspaper"],"why":"He met God there."},{"q":"What does Psalm 119:105 call God's word?","o":["A lamp to my feet","A heavy stone","A secret","A riddle"],"why":"And a light to my path."}]},"ac2s3":{"story":[{"sc":"church","n":"As Ajayi read the Bible and listened to his teachers, he came to believe in Jesus for himself."},{"sc":"church","w":"ajayi","e":"smile","t":"The same God who watched over me in Osogun, in the market and on the ship, he loves me. I want to follow Jesus."},{"sc":"church","n":"On 11 December 1825, Ajayi was baptised. At his baptism he took a new name: Samuel Crowther."},{"sc":"church","n":"Samuel Crowther was the name of an English minister who had helped start the mission that ran his school. Ajayi took his name as a sign of thanks."},{"sc":"church","n":"But he never threw away his Yoruba name. All his life he was Samuel Ajayi Crowther, both names together."},{"sc":"church","w":"youngcrowther","e":"big","t":"I am Samuel Ajayi Crowther. Yoruba, African, and a child of God."},{"sc":"freetown","n":"Think about that. He didn't have to choose between being African and being Christian. He was proudly both."}],"quote":{"text":"And thou shalt be called by a new name, which the mouth of the LORD shall name.","who":"Isaiah 62:2","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Both names","text":"Ajayi became Samuel at his baptism, but he kept his Yoruba name all his life. Following Jesus doesn't mean being ashamed of where you come from. God made your culture and your language. Bring all of who you are to him."},"think":"Why do you think Ajayi kept his Yoruba name alongside his new name? What does your name mean, and what does it say about you?","proverb":{"text":"Ilé là á wò, ká tó sọmọ lórúkọ.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"We look at the home before we name the child. A name carries the story of where you come from."},"quiz":[{"q":"When was Ajayi baptised?","o":["11 December 1825","1 January 1900","25 December 1821","Never"],"why":"In Sierra Leone."},{"q":"What new name did he take?","o":["Samuel Crowther","John Bunyan","Peter Paul","Henry Leeke"],"why":"After an English minister."},{"q":"Why did he choose that name?","o":["As thanks to a man who helped start his school's mission","He liked the sound","It was a king's name","It was random"],"why":"A sign of gratitude."},{"q":"Did he give up his Yoruba name?","o":["No, he was Samuel Ajayi Crowther all his life","Yes, completely","Only at school","He changed it to a number"],"why":"Proudly both."},{"q":"What does the Yoruba proverb about naming mean?","o":["A name carries the story of where you come from","Names don't matter","Change your name often","Only kings have names"],"why":"We look at the home before naming the child."}]},"ac2s4":{"story":[{"sc":"seashore","n":"In 1826, one of his teachers took Samuel on a voyage to England. He went to a school in London for a few months. Everything was new: the cold, the buildings, the crowds."},{"sc":"college","n":"When he came back to Freetown, a new college was opening: {Fourah Bay College}. In 1827, the very first student to enrol was Samuel Ajayi Crowther."},{"sc":"college","w":"youngcrowther","e":"big","t":"The boy who was swapped for a horse is now a college student. Only God could do this."},{"sc":"college","n":"Fourah Bay College went on to train many of West Africa's great leaders. And Samuel was the first."},{"sc":"church","n":"In 1829, Samuel married a young woman named Asano, who had also been rescued from a slave ship. She was baptised as Susan. They were married for many years and had several children."},{"sc":"church","w":"asano","e":"smile","t":"We both know what it is to lose everything. And we both know the God who gave us everything back."},{"sc":"study","n":"Samuel became a teacher himself. The boy who once couldn't read was now teaching other freed children their letters."}],"quote":{"text":"Wisdom is the principal thing; therefore get wisdom: and with all thy getting get understanding.","who":"Proverbs 4:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Learn, then teach","text":"Samuel didn't just learn for himself. As soon as he could, he started teaching others. The best leaders are learners who become teachers, passing on what they've received."},"think":"Who taught you something important? Who could you teach something to this week?","proverb":{"text":"Ọmọ tí a kò kọ́ ni yóò gbé ilé tí a kọ́ tà.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"A child who is not taught will sell the house we built. Education protects the future."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where did Samuel travel in 1826?","o":["England","America","Egypt","India"],"why":"He went to a school in London."},{"q":"What was special about Samuel at Fourah Bay College?","o":["He was the very first student","He was the principal","He built it","He failed"],"why":"In 1827."},{"q":"Who did Samuel marry?","o":["Asano, also rescued from a slave ship","A queen","Mr Davey's daughter","Nobody"],"why":"She was baptised Susan."},{"q":"What job did Samuel do?","o":["He became a teacher","A sailor","A soldier","A trader"],"why":"Teaching freed children their letters."},{"q":"According to Proverbs 4:7, what is the principal thing?","o":["Wisdom","Money","Fame","Strength"],"why":"Therefore get wisdom."}]}}},"ac3":{"challenge":[{"q":"What was the purpose of the 1841 Niger Expedition?","o":["To make friends with rulers, stop the slave trade and explore mission work","To find gold","To fight a war","To go fishing"],"why":"Three British steamships."},{"q":"Which disease struck the expedition?","o":["Malaria","Measles","Cholera","Flu"],"why":"Spread by mosquitoes."},{"q":"Where was Crowther ordained?","o":["London","Freetown","Abeokuta","Lagos"],"why":"In 1843."},{"q":"Why did Crowther's Yoruba preaching move people so much?","o":["They heard God's word in their own language","It was very short","He sang it","He shouted"],"why":"From one of their own people."},{"q":"What books had Crowther written about Yoruba?","o":["A grammar and vocabulary","Novels","Cookbooks","Maps"],"why":"So others could learn it."},{"q":"Which missionary went with Crowther to Abeokuta?","o":["Henry Townsend","John Bunyan","Mr Leeke","Mungo Park"],"why":"In 1846."},{"q":"Abeokuta is built around what?","o":["A giant rock","A lake","A volcano","A palace"],"why":"Olumo Rock."},{"q":"What did his mother do after they were reunited?","o":["Came to trust in Jesus and was baptised","Went back to Osogun","Moved to England","Sold the house"],"why":"She took the name Hannah."},{"q":"Who in the Bible was also called Hannah?","o":["A mother who prayed for her son","A queen of Egypt","A prophet's sister","A fisherman's wife"],"why":"Samuel's mother."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Be strong and of a good ___.\"","o":["courage","heart","food","house"],"why":"Joshua 1:9."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! Samuel's mother took the name Hannah. What was the name of Hannah's son in the Bible?","o":["Samuel","David","Joseph","Moses"],"why":"The same name as her own son, Samuel Ajayi Crowther!"},"choices":[{"title":"Prepare for your moment","q":"You've been asked to join a debate team, but the first competition is months away. Your friends say there's no point practising yet.","o":[{"t":"Start preparing now, little by little, so you're ready.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther had prepared faithfully for years, so he was ready when his moment came."},{"t":"Wait until the week before.","f":"Last-minute cramming rarely makes you ready."},{"t":"Quit. It's too far away to matter.","f":"Faithful preparation matters most when nobody's watching."}]},{"title":"Your mother tongue","q":"Your grandma speaks to you in your mother tongue, but you usually reply in English. Today she looks sad about it.","o":[{"t":"Try to answer her in your language, even if it's not perfect, and ask her to teach you more.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther treasured Yoruba and helped millions read God's word in it."},{"t":"Keep using English. It's easier.","f":"Easy isn't always best. Your language is a treasure."},{"t":"Tell her nobody speaks it any more.","f":"Millions still do, and it matters."}]},{"title":"Back to a hard place","q":"You've been asked to help at a children's programme in the neighbourhood where you used to be bullied.","o":[{"t":"Pray about it, take a friend, and go with a heart to bless.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther went back to the land where he had been enslaved, with good news."},{"t":"Refuse. That place only has bad memories.","f":"That's understandable, but God can turn pain into purpose."},{"t":"Go, but only to show off how well you're doing now.","f":"Crowther went to serve, not to show off."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"niger","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 3 of Ajayi Crowther's story in real life."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"📚 Prepare faithfully now, so you're ready for your moment later."},{"sc":"church","n":"🗣️ Value your mother tongue. God speaks your language."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"🪨 God can turn the place of your worst pain into the place of your greatest work."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"🤱 God sees every tear. He is a restorer."}],"stops":{"ac3s1":{"story":[{"sc":"niger","n":"In 1841, the British government sent three steamships up the {River Niger}, the great river that gives Nigeria its name."},{"sc":"niger","n":"The {expedition} wanted to make friends with African rulers, stop the slave trade, and find out whether missionaries could work there. They chose Samuel Crowther to go with them."},{"sc":"niger","w":"youngcrowther","e":"big","t":"The Niger! I was taken away from this land as a slave. Now I'm coming back as a free man, with good news."},{"sc":"niger","n":"The ships steamed up the river, past forests, towns and markets. Crowther spoke with chiefs and traders, and wrote everything down in his journal."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"But then {malaria} struck. Fever spread from cabin to cabin. Dozens of the European sailors and officers died. The expedition had to turn back."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"Crowther stayed healthy. He nursed the sick, kept writing, and kept his eyes open."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Many people learned a big lesson from that terrible journey: the best people to carry the gospel into Africa were Africans themselves. And Samuel Ajayi Crowther was ready."}],"quote":{"text":"Be strong and of a good courage; be not afraid, neither be thou dismayed: for the LORD thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest.","who":"Joshua 1:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Ready when it's your turn","text":"Everyone else got sick on the Niger. Crowther stayed well, served others and kept learning. Because he had prepared faithfully for years, he was ready when his moment came. Prepare now for what God may call you to later."},"think":"Why do you think it made sense for Africans to lead the mission in Africa? What strengths did Crowther have that the Europeans didn't?","quiz":[{"q":"In which year was the Niger Expedition?","o":["1841","1941","1741","2001"],"why":"Three steamships."},{"q":"What gives Nigeria its name?","o":["The River Niger","A king","A mountain","A lake"],"why":"West Africa's greatest river."},{"q":"What disaster struck the expedition?","o":["Malaria fever","A shipwreck","A war","A snowstorm"],"why":"Many Europeans died."},{"q":"What did Crowther do?","o":["Stayed well, nursed the sick, and kept a journal","Ran away","Got sick and went home","Stayed in Freetown"],"why":"Faithful and ready."},{"q":"What big lesson did people learn?","o":["Africans were the best people to carry the gospel into Africa","Rivers are boring","Steamships are too slow","Africa should be avoided"],"why":"And Crowther was ready."}]},"ac3s2":{"story":[{"sc":"college","n":"In 1842, Crowther was sent to London to train to become a minister of the church. He studied hard, as he always did."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"In 1843 he was {ordained}, one of the first Africans of his time to become a minister in the Church of England."},{"sc":"freetown","n":"He sailed back to Freetown. Many of the people there were freed Yoruba captives, like him. They had heard sermons in English for years."},{"sc":"church","n":"Then Samuel Ajayi Crowther stood up in the pulpit and preached to them in Yoruba, their own language."},{"sc":"church","w":"revcrowther","e":"smile","t":"Ẹ kú àbọ̀, ẹ̀yin ará mi. Welcome, my brothers and sisters. God loves you, and he speaks your language."},{"sc":"church","n":"The people were deeply moved. Many had never heard God's word in their own mother tongue, from one of their own people."},{"sc":"church","n":"Crowther had already written a Yoruba grammar book and vocabulary list, so that others could learn the language too."}],"quote":{"text":"How beautiful are the feet of them that preach the gospel of peace, and bring glad tidings of good things!","who":"Romans 10:15","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God speaks your language","text":"When Crowther preached in Yoruba, people were deeply moved. Hearing God's word in your own language touches your heart in a special way. God isn't foreign. He speaks Yoruba, Igbo, Hausa, English and every language."},"think":"Have you ever heard a song or prayer in your mother tongue that touched you more deeply than in English? Why do you think that is?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did Crowther go to train as a minister?","o":["London","Rome","Lagos","Cairo"],"why":"In 1842."},{"q":"What happened in 1843?","o":["He was ordained a minister","He became a king","He went to prison","He got married"],"why":"One of the first Africans of his time."},{"q":"In what language did he preach in Freetown?","o":["Yoruba","Latin","French","Greek"],"why":"The people's mother tongue."},{"q":"How did the people react?","o":["They were deeply moved","They laughed","They left","They slept"],"why":"God's word in their own language."},{"q":"What had Crowther written to help people learn Yoruba?","o":["A grammar book and vocabulary","A novel","A song","Nothing"],"why":"So others could learn it too."}]},"ac3s3":{"story":[{"sc":"seashore","n":"By the 1840s, many freed Yoruba people in Sierra Leone were sailing home to Nigeria. They were called the {Saro}."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"Many of them settled in {Abeokuta}, a Yoruba city built around a giant rock. Its name means 'under the rock'."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"The Egba chiefs of Abeokuta welcomed the returning Saro and invited missionaries to come. In 1846, Crowther arrived in Abeokuta with a missionary named Henry Townsend."},{"sc":"abeokuta","w":"townsend","e":"smile","t":"Crowther, these are your own people. They'll listen to you as they'd never listen to me."},{"sc":"abeokuta","w":"revcrowther","e":"big","t":"Twenty-five years ago I was dragged from this land in chains. Now God has brought me back to it with good news."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"He built a house, started a school and a church, and began teaching and preaching. Crowds came to hear the Yoruba man who had come home."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"But God had one more gift waiting for him in Abeokuta. A gift he had stopped hoping for long ago."}],"quote":{"text":"Let the redeemed of the LORD say so, whom he hath redeemed from the hand of the enemy.","who":"Psalm 107:2","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Going back to bless","text":"Crowther returned to the land where he had been captured, not with bitterness but with good news. God can turn the place of your worst pain into the place of your greatest work."},"think":"Why might it have been hard for Crowther to go back to the land where he was enslaved? What gave him the strength?","quiz":[{"q":"Who were the Saro?","o":["Freed Yoruba returning home from Sierra Leone","British sailors","Fulani horsemen","Portuguese traders"],"why":"Many settled in Abeokuta."},{"q":"What does 'Abeokuta' mean?","o":["Under the rock","City of kings","River town","New home"],"why":"It's built around Olumo Rock."},{"q":"In what year did Crowther arrive in Abeokuta?","o":["1846","1821","1900","1864"],"why":"With Henry Townsend."},{"q":"What did Crowther start in Abeokuta?","o":["A school and a church","A war","A slave market","A bank"],"why":"Teaching and preaching."},{"q":"How long had it been since he was taken from this land?","o":["About twenty-five years","Two years","One hundred years","Six months"],"why":"Now he came back with good news."}]},"ac3s4":{"story":[{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"One day in August 1846, some people came to Crowther with astonishing news."},{"sc":"abeokuta","w":"revcrowther","e":"shock","t":"My mother? Alive? Here in Abeokuta?"},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"Afala, his mother, had also been sold into slavery after the raid. But she had stayed in Yorubaland, and somehow she had survived all those years. Now she was brought to him."},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"Crowther wrote about that moment in his journal:"},{"sc":"abeokuta","w":"oldafala","e":"shock","t":"Ajayi? My son? Is it really you?"},{"sc":"abeokuta","n":"He wrote that when she saw him she trembled, and could not believe her own eyes. They held each other in silence, with big tears rolling down her cheeks."},{"sc":"abeokuta","w":"revcrowther","e":"big","t":"Mama! God has kept you! After twenty-five years, God has brought us together!"},{"sc":"church","n":"His mother came to live with him. She listened to her son's preaching, and she too came to trust in Jesus. In 1848 she was baptised and took the name Hannah."},{"sc":"church","n":"In the Bible, Hannah was a mother who prayed for her son and gave him to God. Now Ajayi's mother had her son back, and she gave her own life to God."}],"quote":{"text":"When she saw me she trembled. She could not believe her own eyes.","who":"Crowther's journal, August 1846","tag":"Crowther's words"},"lesson":{"title":"God restores","text":"Twenty-five years after a raid tore them apart, God brought Ajayi and his mother together again. Not every loss is restored in this life, but God is a restorer. He sees every tear, and he keeps his children."},"think":"Imagine being separated from your mother for twenty-five years. How do you think Crowther felt? What does this story show you about God?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Crowther find his mother?","o":["August 1846","June 1822","December 1825","1891"],"why":"In Abeokuta."},{"q":"How long had they been apart?","o":["About twenty-five years","Two years","Fifty years","One year"],"why":"Since the raid on Osogun."},{"q":"How did his mother react when she saw him?","o":["She trembled and couldn't believe her eyes","She didn't recognise him","She ran away","She laughed"],"why":"Big tears rolled down her cheeks."},{"q":"What name did she take at her baptism?","o":["Hannah","Mary","Susan","Esther"],"why":"In 1848."},{"q":"What does Psalm 126:5 promise?","o":["They that sow in tears shall reap in joy","Tears last for ever","Joy never comes","Don't cry"],"why":"God turns tears into joy."}]}}},"ac4":{"challenge":[{"q":"What did Crowther help Yoruba to have?","o":["A way of writing it in letters","A new grammar for English","A king","A flag"],"why":"With dots and tone marks."},{"q":"Which letters have dots under them in Yoruba?","o":["ẹ, ọ and ṣ","a, b and c","x, y and z","None"],"why":"For special sounds."},{"q":"In what year was the whole Yoruba Bible finished?","o":["1884","1846","1964","1707"],"why":"After decades of work."},{"q":"Where did Crowther meet Queen Victoria?","o":["Windsor","Lagos","Abeokuta","Freetown"],"why":"In 1851."},{"q":"What did Crowther ask Britain to stop?","o":["The slave trade at Lagos","Trade with Africa","Missionaries","Schools"],"why":"It was still tearing families apart."},{"q":"Where was the Dayspring wrecked?","o":["Near Jebba on the Niger","Off Lagos","In the Thames","On Lake Chad"],"why":"On hidden rocks."},{"q":"What did Crowther do while stranded?","o":["Learned Nupe and made friends","Complained","Slept","Went home on foot"],"why":"Bloom where you're stuck."},{"q":"Which town was the first station of the Niger Mission?","o":["Onitsha","Ibadan","Zaria","Calabar"],"why":"A great market town."},{"q":"Which of these towns had a church in the Niger Mission?","o":["Lokoja","Paris","Cairo","Accra"],"why":"Along with Onitsha and Bonny."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"All things work together for ___ to them that love God.\"","o":["good","money","fun","nothing"],"why":"Romans 8:28."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What prayer did Crowther say in Yoruba for Queen Victoria?","o":["The Lord's Prayer","A war cry","The Yoruba national anthem","A birthday song"],"why":"God's word in his mother tongue, before a queen."},"choices":[{"title":"Plans wrecked","q":"You were chosen for the inter-school competition, but you broke your arm a week before. You're stuck at home for a month.","o":[{"t":"Ask God what you can do right now: read, learn a skill, help at home, cheer your team.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther turned a shipwreck into a year of learning."},{"t":"Sulk for the whole month.","f":"Understandable, but that wastes the time God gave you."},{"t":"Blame everyone around you.","f":"Romans 8:28: God can bring good even from this."}]},{"title":"Using your voice","q":"Your school is collecting views from pupils for the principal. You know that some younger pupils are being bullied at the bus stop.","o":[{"t":"Use your chance to speak up for them clearly and respectfully.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther used his time with the Queen to speak for those still suffering."},{"t":"Only ask for things you want, like longer breaks.","f":"Platforms are for others too."},{"t":"Say nothing. It's not your business.","f":"Your voice could protect someone."}]},{"title":"Slow, faithful work","q":"You've started learning to play the piano, but after two weeks you still can't play a full song. You want to quit.","o":[{"t":"Keep practising a little every day. Big things are built slowly.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! The Yoruba Bible took decades of verse-by-verse work."},{"t":"Quit. You're obviously not talented.","f":"Talent grows with patience."},{"t":"Only practise when you feel like it.","f":"Faithful work beats moods."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"study","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 4 of Ajayi Crowther's story in real life."},{"sc":"letterdesk","n":"✍️ Don't despise slow, faithful work. It can bless millions."},{"sc":"palace","n":"📣 When you get a platform, speak up for those who don't have a voice."},{"sc":"niger","n":"🚢 When your plans are wrecked, ask: what can I do right here, right now?"},{"sc":"market","n":"🌍 Every language and people matters to God. Celebrate them."}],"stops":{"ac4s1":{"story":[{"sc":"study","n":"Here's a question. How do you write a language that has never been written in letters before?"},{"sc":"study","n":"That's the problem Crowther faced. Yoruba was a rich, beautiful language full of proverbs, songs and stories. But almost nothing had been written down in it."},{"sc":"study","w":"revcrowther","e":"worried","t":"Yoruba has sounds that English letters don't have. And the same word can mean different things depending on whether your voice goes up or down."},{"sc":"study","n":"So Crowther and his fellow workers helped work out a way to write it. Dots under some letters for special sounds, like ẹ, ọ and ṣ. Marks above letters to show the tones."},{"sc":"study","n":"If you've ever written Yoruba with those little dots and marks, you're using the system that Crowther helped to create!"},{"sc":"letterdesk","n":"Then he started the biggest job of all: helping to {translate} the Bible into Yoruba. Book by book, chapter by chapter, verse by verse, for years."},{"sc":"church","n":"Other translators worked with him. Little by little, the books of the Bible were printed. The whole Yoruba Bible, the {Bíbélì Mímọ́}, was finally finished in 1884."},{"sc":"church","n":"Millions of people have read God's word in Yoruba since then. And it all started with a slave boy who learned to read in six months."}],"quote":{"text":"And how hear we every man in our own tongue, wherein we were born?","who":"Acts 2:8","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Patient, faithful work","text":"Translating the Bible took decades of slow, careful work, verse by verse. Some of the most important things in life are built slowly. Don't despise small, steady, faithful work. It can bless millions."},"think":"Why do you think it mattered so much for Yoruba people to have the Bible in their own language?","proverb":{"text":"Ilimi gishirin zaman duniya.","lang":"Hausa","mean":"Knowledge is the salt of life. Learning gives life its flavour."},"quiz":[{"q":"What problem did Crowther face with Yoruba?","o":["It had hardly ever been written in letters","Nobody spoke it","It was too short","It had no words for God"],"why":"A rich spoken language."},{"q":"What do the dots under some Yoruba letters show?","o":["Special sounds","Mistakes","Full stops","Numbers"],"why":"Like ẹ, ọ and ṣ."},{"q":"Why does Yoruba need marks above letters?","o":["To show the tones","For decoration","To show capitals","To show the date"],"why":"The same word can mean different things."},{"q":"When was the whole Yoruba Bible finished?","o":["1884","1821","1999","1611"],"why":"After many years of work."},{"q":"What does 'Bíbélì Mímọ́' mean?","o":["Holy Bible","Good morning","Thank you","Big river"],"why":"In Yoruba."}]},"ac4s2":{"story":[{"sc":"cathedral","n":"In 1851, Crowther travelled to England again. He had an important message: the slave trade was still going on at Lagos, and it had to be stopped."},{"sc":"palace","n":"He met important government leaders. And then he was invited to {Windsor}, to meet Queen Victoria and her husband, Prince Albert."},{"sc":"palace","n":"Imagine it. The boy who had been sold for a horse at a Yoruba market was now standing before the Queen of the most powerful empire in the world."},{"sc":"palace","w":"queenvictoria","e":"smile","t":"Mr Crowther, tell us about your country."},{"sc":"palace","w":"revcrowther","e":"smile","t":"Your Majesty, the Yoruba people want peace and trade. But at Lagos, the slave trade still tears families apart, as it tore mine."},{"sc":"palace","n":"He showed them where the towns were on a map, and explained what was happening. He even said the Lord's Prayer for them in Yoruba."},{"sc":"palace","n":"Not long afterwards, Britain took action to end the slave trade at Lagos. Crowther's courage to speak up had helped."}],"quote":{"text":"Seest thou a man diligent in his business? he shall stand before kings; he shall not stand before mean men.","who":"Proverbs 22:29","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Speak up for others","text":"When Crowther stood before the Queen, he didn't talk about himself. He spoke up for the people still suffering. When God gives you a platform, use it for those who don't have a voice."},"think":"If you could speak to a president or a queen for five minutes, what injustice would you ask them to fix?","quiz":[{"q":"Why did Crowther go to England in 1851?","o":["To ask for the slave trade at Lagos to be stopped","To go on holiday","To buy a ship","To study medicine"],"why":"He had an important message."},{"q":"Who did he meet at Windsor?","o":["Queen Victoria and Prince Albert","Nero","Napoleon","Nobody"],"why":"The Queen of a great empire."},{"q":"What did he say for them in Yoruba?","o":["The Lord's Prayer","A joke","A poem about horses","The alphabet"],"why":"In his mother tongue."},{"q":"What happened not long afterwards?","o":["Britain took action against the slave trade at Lagos","Nothing","The Queen moved to Lagos","Crowther became king"],"why":"His courage helped."},{"q":"According to Proverbs 22:29, where will a diligent person stand?","o":["Before kings","In the back","Alone","In the rain"],"why":"Diligence opens doors."}]},"ac4s3":{"story":[{"sc":"niger","n":"In 1857, Crowther went up the Niger again, on a steamship called the Dayspring."},{"sc":"shipstorm","n":"Near the town of {Jebba}, the river was full of hidden rocks. With a terrible crunch, the Dayspring struck a rock and was wrecked."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Nobody was killed. But the crew and passengers were stranded, far up the river. It took about a year before help arrived."},{"sc":"niger","w":"revcrowther","e":"smile","t":"A shipwreck is not the end of the work. If we are stuck here, then here is where God wants us to work."},{"sc":"niger","n":"So Crowther didn't sit around complaining. He learned the {Nupe} language. He visited towns and markets. He talked with chiefs and made friends."},{"sc":"niger","w":"nupechief","e":"smile","t":"You speak our language now, Crowther? Come, sit with us. Tell us about this God of yours."},{"sc":"niger","n":"By the time the rescue ship came, Crowther knew the area and its people far better than he would have if the ship had never been wrecked."},{"sc":"niger","n":"A disaster had become an opportunity. That's how God often works."}],"quote":{"text":"And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose.","who":"Romans 8:28","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Bloom where you're stuck","text":"The shipwreck at Jebba could have been a wasted year. Crowther turned it into a year of learning and friendship. When your plans are wrecked, ask: what can I do right here, right now?"},"think":"Think of a time your plans were 'wrecked'. How could you have used that time well, like Crowther at Jebba?","quiz":[{"q":"What was the name of the ship wrecked at Jebba?","o":["The Dayspring","The Esperança Felix","The Myrmidon","The Albert"],"why":"In 1857."},{"q":"What caused the wreck?","o":["Hidden rocks in the river","A storm","Pirates","A whale"],"why":"A terrible crunch."},{"q":"How long were they stranded?","o":["About a year","One day","Ten years","A week"],"why":"Before help arrived."},{"q":"What language did Crowther learn while stranded?","o":["Nupe","French","Latin","Chinese"],"why":"He made friends with the people."},{"q":"What does Romans 8:28 say?","o":["All things work together for good to them that love God","Bad things never happen","Luck decides everything","Give up when things go wrong"],"why":"A disaster became an opportunity."}]},"ac4s4":{"story":[{"sc":"niger","n":"In 1857, Crowther started the {Niger Mission}. Its first station was at {Onitsha}, the great market town on the river."},{"sc":"market","w":"jctaylor","e":"smile","t":"Mr Crowther, the people here speak Igbo. I can teach them in their own language."},{"sc":"market","n":"John Christopher Taylor was born in Sierra Leone to freed Igbo parents. Now he had come to his parents' homeland to start the work at Onitsha."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Something was special about the Niger Mission. Almost all of its teachers, preachers and pastors were Africans: Yoruba, Igbo, Nupe and others."},{"sc":"study","n":"Crowther made sure the mission had books in the people's own languages: reading books in Igbo, Nupe and other languages, as well as Yoruba."},{"sc":"college","n":"Schools were opened. Churches were built at towns along the river: Onitsha, Lokoja, Bonny and more."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Crowther believed every people and every language matters to God. In heaven, the Bible says, there will be people from every tongue, praising God together."}],"quote":{"text":"After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne.","who":"Revelation 7:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Every language matters","text":"Crowther made sure Igbo, Nupe and Yoruba speakers could hear God's word in their own languages, from their own people. God loves every tribe and every tongue. Respect and celebrate the languages and cultures around you."},"think":"Nigeria has more than 500 languages. Why do you think every one of them matters to God?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did the Niger Mission begin?","o":["Onitsha","London","Kano","Freetown"],"why":"In 1857."},{"q":"Who started the work at Onitsha?","o":["John Christopher Taylor, an Igbo Christian","Queen Victoria","Henry Townsend","Mr Davey"],"why":"Born in Sierra Leone to freed Igbo parents."},{"q":"What was special about the Niger Mission's workers?","o":["Almost all were Africans","They were all soldiers","They were all children","They were all British"],"why":"Yoruba, Igbo, Nupe and others."},{"q":"In which languages did Crowther provide books?","o":["Igbo, Nupe and Yoruba, among others","Only English","Only Latin","None"],"why":"The people's own languages."},{"q":"Who will stand before the throne in Revelation 7:9?","o":["People of all nations and tongues","Only one nation","Only kings","Nobody"],"why":"Every language matters."}]}}},"ac5":{"challenge":[{"q":"Where was Crowther consecrated as a bishop?","o":["Canterbury Cathedral","Abeokuta","Freetown","Westminster"],"why":"On 29 June 1864."},{"q":"Crowther was the first African what?","o":["Anglican bishop","King of England","Pilot","Footballer"],"why":"A historic first."},{"q":"What did Oxford University give him?","o":["An honorary doctorate","A ship","A house","A medal for running"],"why":"Dr Crowther!"},{"q":"How did Bishop Crowther usually travel?","o":["By steamer, canoe and on foot","By car","By horse-drawn coach","He stayed home"],"why":"A servant bishop."},{"q":"Which of his children became a church leader?","o":["Dandeson","Samuel Jr.","Ajayi Jr.","Hannah"],"why":"In the Niger Delta."},{"q":"Who treated the African pastors unfairly in the 1880s?","o":["Some young European missionaries","Queen Victoria","The Nupe chiefs","His mother"],"why":"Prejudice."},{"q":"Where did Crowther die?","o":["Lagos","London","Osogun","Onitsha"],"why":"On 31 December 1891."},{"q":"Which writing system did Crowther help create?","o":["Yoruba with dots and tone marks","Chinese characters","Morse code","Braille"],"why":"Still used today."},{"q":"How long after he died does Crowther's legacy still bless people?","o":["More than 130 years and counting","One year","It doesn't","Ten minutes"],"why":"His work lives on."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Whosoever will be great among you, shall be your ___.\"","o":["minister","king","boss","judge"],"why":"Mark 10:43."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! Put Ajayi's life in order: which came FIRST?","o":["Captured at Osogun","Became a bishop","Met Queen Victoria","Found his mother"],"why":"1821: the start of an amazing journey."},"choices":[{"title":"A title goes to your head","q":"You've just been made class prefect. Some friends expect you to stop doing your share of the cleaning now.","o":[{"t":"Keep doing your share, and help others too.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Bishop Crowther slept in huts and paddled canoes. Great leaders serve."},{"t":"Boss everyone around. You earned it.","f":"Jesus said the greatest is the servant of all."},{"t":"Only help when the teacher is watching.","f":"Servant leadership doesn't need an audience."}]},{"title":"Prejudice","q":"A new student is told by some classmates that people from his tribe are 'lazy' and can't be trusted with leadership.","o":[{"t":"Speak up kindly: judge people by their character, not their tribe. Include him.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther's African pastors were judged unfairly too."},{"t":"Agree, so you fit in.","f":"That's the prejudice that hurt Crowther."},{"t":"Stay silent.","f":"Silence can let prejudice grow."}]},{"title":"Wronged and angry","q":"A teacher gave you a lower mark than you deserved and wouldn't listen when you tried to explain.","o":[{"t":"Stay calm, ask respectfully for another look, and leave the rest to God.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther answered unfairness with dignity, not bitterness."},{"t":"Insult the teacher behind their back.","f":"That doesn't change the mark, and it shrinks you."},{"t":"Give up working in that subject.","f":"Don't let unfairness steal your future."}]},{"title":"Your legacy","q":"Someone asks you, \"What do you want to be remembered for?\"","o":[{"t":"Think about it seriously, and start one small thing now that blesses others.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Crowther's legacy started with a boy learning to read."},{"t":"Say, \"Being rich and famous.\"","f":"Crowther is remembered for serving, not for money."},{"t":"Say it doesn't matter.","f":"Your life matters more than you know."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"cathedral","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's how you'll use Samuel Ajayi Crowther's story for the rest of your life."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"⬆️ God lifts people up from the lowest places. Stay humble when he lifts you."},{"sc":"niger","n":"🛶 Great leaders serve. Never let a title make you proud."},{"sc":"niger","n":"🕊️ When you're treated unfairly, answer with dignity and trust God to judge justly."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"🌟 What others mean for evil, God can turn to good. Start building your legacy now."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"🇳🇬 Well done! You've finished the story of a true Nigerian hero."}],"stops":{"ac5s1":{"story":[{"sc":"cathedral","n":"On 29 June 1864, crowds filled {Canterbury Cathedral} in England. Something historic was about to happen."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"Samuel Ajayi Crowther, now in his fifties, walked slowly down the aisle. That day he was {consecrated} as a {bishop}: the first African bishop of the Anglican Church."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"Think back. A boy in Osogun. A town on fire. Ropes around his neck. Sold for a horse. Chained in a slave ship. And now, a bishop."},{"sc":"cathedral","w":"bishopcrowther","e":"smile","t":"This is not my doing. It is the Lord's doing, and it is marvellous in our eyes."},{"sc":"college","n":"That same year, Oxford University, one of the oldest universities in the world, gave him an {honorary doctorate}. The boy who first held a book at about thirteen was now Dr Crowther."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"He was made bishop of the lands along the Niger and in West Africa. It was a huge area, with no roads and few churches. But Crowther had never been afraid of hard work."},{"sc":"cathedral","n":"God lifted Ajayi from the lowest place to one of the highest, not so he could be proud, but so he could serve."}],"quote":{"text":"He raiseth up the poor out of the dust, and lifteth up the beggar from the dunghill, to set them among princes.","who":"1 Samuel 2:8","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God lifts up","text":"From a slave ship to Canterbury Cathedral: only God could write that story. Crowther knew it, and he gave God the glory. Wherever you start in life, God can lift you up. And when he does, stay humble."},"think":"Crowther said, 'This is the Lord's doing.' Why is it important to stay humble when good things happen to us?","quiz":[{"q":"When was Crowther made a bishop?","o":["29 June 1864","11 December 1825","1 January 1900","June 1822"],"why":"In Canterbury Cathedral."},{"q":"What was historic about it?","o":["He was the first African bishop of the Anglican Church","He was the youngest","He was a king","It was in Lagos"],"why":"From slave ship to bishop."},{"q":"Which university gave him an honorary doctorate?","o":["Oxford","Harvard","Ibadan","Cambridge"],"why":"In 1864."},{"q":"Who did Crowther say had done this?","o":["The Lord","Himself","The Queen","Luck"],"why":"It is the Lord's doing."},{"q":"What does 1 Samuel 2:8 say God does for the poor?","o":["Raises them up and sets them among princes","Forgets them","Keeps them down","Ignores them"],"why":"God lifts up."}]},"ac5s2":{"story":[{"sc":"niger","n":"Being a bishop didn't mean sitting on a throne. For Crowther, it meant endless travelling: by steamer, by canoe and on foot, up and down the Niger."},{"sc":"niger","w":"bishopcrowther","e":"smile","t":"Come, paddle on. There's a village up the creek that has never heard about Jesus."},{"sc":"niger","n":"He slept in simple huts. He ate whatever the villagers ate. He sat with chiefs, with traders, with children and with slaves."},{"sc":"college","n":"He trained African pastors and teachers, and gave them real responsibility. One of them was his own son, Dandeson Crowther, who became a church leader in the Niger Delta."},{"sc":"college","w":"dandeson","e":"smile","t":"Father, you never ask us to do anything you won't do yourself."},{"sc":"niger","n":"People who met him said he was gentle, patient and humble. Even as an old man in his seventies, he was still travelling the rivers to visit his churches."},{"sc":"niger","n":"That's what a servant leader looks like: not someone who is served, but someone who serves."}],"quote":{"text":"But whosoever will be great among you, shall be your minister: and whosoever of you will be the chiefest, shall be servant of all.","who":"Mark 10:43-44","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Great leaders serve","text":"Bishop Crowther travelled by canoe, slept in huts, and trained others to lead. He never let his title make him proud. Real greatness, Jesus said, is serving others."},"think":"Dandeson said his father never asked others to do what he wouldn't do himself. Why does that make people want to follow a leader?","quiz":[{"q":"How did Bishop Crowther travel?","o":["By steamer, canoe and on foot","By aeroplane","By train","He never travelled"],"why":"Up and down the Niger."},{"q":"Where did he sleep on his journeys?","o":["In simple huts","In palaces","In hotels","On ships only"],"why":"He lived like the people."},{"q":"Who was Dandeson Crowther?","o":["His son, who became a church leader","His teacher","A slave trader","A king"],"why":"He trained African leaders."},{"q":"How old was Crowther when he was still travelling the rivers?","o":["In his seventies","Twenty","Forty","Ninety-nine"],"why":"Still serving."},{"q":"According to Jesus, who is greatest?","o":["The servant of all","The richest","The strongest","The loudest"],"why":"Mark 10:43-44."}]},"ac5s3":{"story":[{"sc":"niger","n":"Crowther's story has a sad part too. In the 1880s, when he was very old, some young European missionaries arrived on the Niger."},{"sc":"niger","w":"youngmissionary","e":"frown","t":"These African pastors aren't good enough. We should take over and run things properly."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Some of them looked down on the African pastors Crowther had trained. They criticised them harshly, pushed several of them out, and took control of much of his work."},{"sc":"niger","n":"It was deeply unfair. It hurt Crowther badly. He had given his whole life to the mission, and now he watched others undo his work."},{"sc":"niger","w":"bishopcrowther","e":"worried","t":"Lord, you know the truth. I leave it in your hands."},{"sc":"niger","n":"But Crowther did not curse them, or become bitter. He answered with dignity and kept serving as best he could."},{"sc":"home","n":"On 31 December 1891, Samuel Ajayi Crowther died in Lagos, the same city where, seventy years earlier, he had been put on a slave ship."},{"sc":"church","n":"Later generations recognised that he had been treated unfairly. History remembers him not as a failure, but as one of Africa's greatest Christian leaders."}],"quote":{"text":"Who, when he was reviled, reviled not again; when he suffered, he threatened not; but committed himself to him that judgeth righteously.","who":"1 Peter 2:23","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Dignity when wronged","text":"Crowther was treated unfairly because of prejudice. He was hurt, but he didn't become bitter. He left the judging to God, and history proved him right. When you're wronged, you can speak the truth calmly and trust God to judge justly."},"think":"Why do you think some young missionaries looked down on African leaders? How can we stand against prejudice today, while keeping our dignity?","proverb":{"text":"Ẹni tí ó bá fẹ́ jẹ oyin inú àpáta, kì í wo ẹnu àáké.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"Whoever wants to eat the honey inside the rock doesn't worry about the edge of the axe. Great work costs a lot, so keep going."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happened in the 1880s?","o":["Some young European missionaries treated African pastors unfairly","Crowther became king","The Niger dried up","He retired to England"],"why":"It was deeply unfair."},{"q":"How did Crowther respond?","o":["With dignity, without bitterness","He cursed them","He fought them","He quit God"],"why":"He left the judging to God."},{"q":"When did Crowther die?","o":["31 December 1891","29 June 1864","1 January 1821","1946"],"why":"In Lagos."},{"q":"What was special about where he died?","o":["Lagos, where he had been put on a slave ship seventy years earlier","London","Osogun","Freetown"],"why":"His story came full circle."},{"q":"What does 1 Peter 2:23 say Jesus did when insulted?","o":["Did not insult back, but trusted God","Insulted back","Ran away","Called soldiers"],"why":"Committed himself to him that judgeth righteously."}]},"ac5s4":{"story":[{"sc":"osogun","n":"So what did Samuel Ajayi Crowther leave behind? His {legacy} is everywhere in Nigeria today."},{"sc":"study","n":"Every time someone reads the Yoruba Bible, they are reading words he helped bring into their language."},{"sc":"study","n":"Every time someone writes Yoruba with those dots and tone marks, they're using a system he helped to create."},{"sc":"church","n":"Churches along the Niger, in Onitsha, Lokoja and the Delta, trace their beginnings to the mission he led."},{"sc":"college","n":"There is a university named after him: Ajayi Crowther University, in Oyo, not far from where he was born."},{"sc":"osogun","n":"Most of all, his life shows what God can do with one person. The raiders who burned Osogun meant it for evil. The traders who sold him meant it for evil. But God meant it for good."},{"sc":"osogun","w":"bishopcrowther","e":"smile","t":"God took a slave boy from Osogun and made him a messenger to his own people. If he can do that with me, think what he can do with you."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"Samuel Ajayi Crowther: Yoruba boy, freed slave, scholar, translator, explorer, bishop, servant of God. A true Nigerian hero."}],"quote":{"text":"But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive.","who":"Genesis 50:20","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"What will you leave?","text":"Crowther's life still blesses millions, 130 years after he died. God turned what others meant for evil into good. Your life can leave a legacy too: in the people you help, the things you build, and the faith you pass on."},"think":"What would you like your legacy to be? What could you start doing now to build it?","quiz":[{"q":"Which Bible did Crowther help bring into being?","o":["The Yoruba Bible","The Latin Bible","The French Bible","None"],"why":"Read by millions."},{"q":"What university is named after him?","o":["Ajayi Crowther University in Oyo","Oxford","University of Lagos","Harvard"],"why":"Near where he was born."},{"q":"Which Bible character also said 'God meant it unto good'?","o":["Joseph","Goliath","Pharaoh","Jonah"],"why":"Sold by his brothers, then raised up to save many."},{"q":"What did God turn the raid and the slave trade into, in Crowther's life?","o":["Good, for many people","Nothing","More evil","A war"],"why":"Genesis 50:20."},{"q":"What is a legacy?","o":["What a person leaves behind that keeps blessing others","A kind of boat","A type of money","A family name"],"why":"Crowther's legacy is everywhere in Nigeria."}]}}}});
